          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"regions\":{\"r2\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"mortals the dice-pieces ( \u0938\u093e\u0930 Mar\u00e2thi \u0938\u094b\u0902\u0917\u091f\u094d\u092f\u093e, ) and day and\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"night the two dice. S'iva may also be regarded as the Destroyer\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"as he is described in the Pur\u00e1\u1e47as as playing at dice with his\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"wife, P\u00e0rvat\u00ee.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Marathi word and the printed accents are difficult to read.\"},\"r3\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 44. \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092a\u093e\u0930\u0948\u0903\u2014avocations, affairs. \u0917\u0941\u0930\u0941-momentous, weighty.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"\u0915\u093e\u0932\u0903\u2014Time, i. e. the passing away of time. \u0924\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0938\u0903\u2014fear or\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"disgust. \u091c\u0928\u094d\u092e &c.\u2014a comp. of the \u0938\u092e\u093e\u0939\u093e\u0930\u0926\u094d\u0935\u0902\u0926\u094d\u0935 class. \u092e\u094b\u0939\u092e\u092f\u0940\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"\u092e\u094b\u0939\u092a\u094d\u0930\u091a\u0941\u0930\u093e\u0902 the aff. \u092e\u092f is used in the sense of \u2018excess\u2019 ( or made\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"of ). \u092e\u0926\u093f\u0930\u093e\u2014derived from \u092e\u0926\u094d+\u0915\u093f\u0930\u091a\u094d ( \u0907\u0930 )+\u091f\u093e\u092a\u094d ( \u0906 )\u2014that\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"which intoxicates. \u0909\u0928\u094d\u092e\u0924\u094d\u0924\u092d\u0942\u0924\u092e\u094d\u2014\u0909\u0928\u094d\u092e\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0902 \u092d\u0942\u0924\u0902; there is no special\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"propriety of taking \u092d\u0942\u0924 in the sense of \u0907\u0935. The poet makes a\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"definite assertion here.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Sanskrit forms, particularly the derivation of \u092e\u0926\u093f\u0930\u093e, are difficult to read.\"},\"r4\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 45. \u0928\u093f\u092d\u0943\u0924\u092e\u094d\u2014resolutely, steadily, or secretly. \u092a\u0941\u0928\u0930\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0924\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"i. e. repeatedly enjoyed, hence stale \u0915\u0926\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093f\u0924\u093e\u0903\u2014disgraced; or\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"tormented. See Nit. s 84; and Infra, \u0915\u0926\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0928\u092e\u094d Mis. Sl. 29.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit words and the reference after Nit. are somewhat unclear.\"},\"r5\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 46. \u0938\u0902\u0938\u093e\u0930\u0935\u093f\u091a\u094d\u091b\u093f\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f\u2014Cutting asunder, or utter destruction,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"of the bonds of \u0938\u0902\u0938\u093e\u0930, leading to moksha ( salvation or unification\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"with the Supreme Being). This is attained by means of Yoga\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"and then there is no re-birth for man. This is the highest\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"ideal; the next one is \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0917, This is attained by means of\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"religious merit, vows, sacrifices &c ; see com. The reward for\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"these is \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0917, and after the heavenly happiness is enjoyed for\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"the prescribed term, there is again rebirth, again the acquisition\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"of \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0917 by religious merit, again re-birth, and so on till the end\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":10,\"text\":\"of the creation. \u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0948\u0903\u2014see com. The student can easily see the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":11,\"text\":\"difference between \u0938\u0902\u0938\u093e\u0930\u0935\u093f\u091a\u094d\u091b\u093f\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f and \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0917\u092a\u094d\u0930\u093e\u092a\u094d\u0924\u093f. In the eyes of\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":12,\"text\":\"a philosophes, the latter is nothing ( \u0924\u0941\u091a\u094d\u091b ) as compared to the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":13,\"text\":\"former. \u0930\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0903\u2014This refers to the third object of human\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":14,\"text\":\"pursuit, viz. \u0915\u093e\u092e or the enjoyment of worldly pleasures. \u092f\u094c\u0935\u0928\u0935\u0928\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":15,\"text\":\"\u2014Not a happy metaphor, as \u0935\u0928 expresses a plurality of ideas.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":16,\"text\":\"It is probably used for alliteration. Or the poet may seem\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":17,\"text\":\"to refer by Vana to an avenue of trees which gradually disap-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":18,\"text\":\"pears as tree after tree is cut down and so to refer to the gradual\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":19,\"text\":\"disappearance of youth. \u0935\u092f\u092e\u094d\u2014The plural is used to indicate\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":20,\"text\":\"self-disparagement.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Sanskrit forms are unclear, especially the headword on line 13.\"},\"r1\":{\"type\":\"page_number\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"( 64 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null}},\"units\":[{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"notes\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"u2\",\"u3\",\"u4\",\"u5\"]},{\"id\":\"u2\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r2\"]},{\"id\":\"u3\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"44\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r3\"]},{\"id\":\"u4\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"45\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r4\"]},{\"id\":\"u5\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"46\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r5\"]}],\"flows\":[{\"flow\":\"main\",\"units\":[\"u1\"]}],\"relations\":[]}"
        }
